hey're too sensitive to potential  scenarios for house prices they the core  task of this bailout is going to be  valuing these mortgage securities and  that is an impossible task and that the  bailout is bound to fail for that reason  it emphasized that none of the  modifications that are being talked  about to this plan whether the Dodd  modifications or what Paul Krugman might  propose make it any better you know  there's been a phrase used in this  political campaign that if you put  lipstick on a pig it's still a pig this  plot any plan that involves this much  involvement of government and finance  and linking them together is a pig no  matter what kind of lipstick you put  onto it  so am i concern that Congress has  seven days to go over it i don't know  whether I'd be more worried if they had  seven days or if they had 50 days the  way Paulson and Bernanke have positioned  it they have no negotiating leverage  whatever Congress adds to it Bernanke  and Paulson are saying we have to  approve sign the bill or it will be the  end of the world so Congress could add  whatever they want they could add  universal health care universal daycare  universal yoga classes I mean whatever  they want to add to this bill and the  bush will have to sign it or else he's  saying that he's committed to the end of  the world  what I what I've suggested as an  alternative is to modify capital  requirements one of the reasons that  financial markets have frozen up is that  banks or especially but other financial  institutions are but especially banks  are struggling to meet regulatory  capital requirements as the mortgage  assets get marked down to lower and  lower values if we were to loosen the  capital requirements they could continue  lending which is the alleged goal of the  Paulson bailout plan but we would not be  committed to trying to bail them out by  buying out these securities without  knowing what their value is their value  can be discovered over time in the  marketplace and if we loosen capital  requirements we're taking a risk that  some banks will fail and will have to  bail them out but that seems like a  smaller more manageable risk we have an  FDIC that's very experienced at  monitoring banks and trying to control  losses and so we'd be building on stuff  that we know that works rather than  spending seven hundred billion dollars  on an untried new business model
 

